Meditation has been shown in numerous studies to lower cortisol levels, a key stress hormone, thereby reducing the physiological impact of stress.
By training the mind to focus and stay present, meditation helps individuals develop better coping mechanisms for stress.
Research indicates that regular meditation practice can increase gray matter in brain regions associated with emotional regulation and resilience.
Many individuals who practice meditation report improved sleep and reduced anxiety, which are common stress-related symptoms.
The practice of mindfulness, a form of meditation, has been integrated into therapeutic settings to help patients manage chronic stress effectively.